<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Configuration for FallowAccessibilityService, referenced from the
  <meta-data> tag in this module's AndroidManifest.xml.

  accessibilityEventTypes — which events the system delivers to
    onAccessibilityEvent. typeWindowStateChanged fires when the foreground
    window/activity changes and drives app/screen blocking.
    typeWindowContentChanged fires whenever anything inside the current
    window updates — needed for website blocking, because navigating within
    a browser never changes windows. It fires very frequently, so the
    service ignores it for every package except known browsers (see
    BROWSER_URL_BARS in FallowAccessibilityService.kt).
    NOTE: some in-app navigation (e.g. swiping into a Reels-style pager)
    also only emits typeWindowContentChanged; if a *screen* rule never
    triggers, the fix is to also run the screen-rule handler on
    content-changed events for that package — see onAccessibilityEvent.

  accessibilityFeedbackType — how the service claims to assist the user;
    used by the system to group services in settings. feedbackGeneric is
    the catch-all for services that don't provide spoken/haptic/etc.
    feedback.

  accessibilityFlags —
    flagDefault: baseline behaviour.
    flagReportViewIds: without this, viewIdResourceName is null on every
      node and findAccessibilityNodeInfosByViewId finds nothing — the
      entire viewIds half of the blocklist depends on this flag.
    flagIncludeNotImportantViews: also report views marked "not important
      for accessibility". Container views (pagers, layouts) — exactly the
      kind of view that identifies a screen — are often in that category.

  canRetrieveWindowContent — grants access to the window's node tree
    (rootInActiveWindow). Without it we could see events but never inspect
    what is on screen. This is the attribute that makes Android show the
    scary "can read screen content" warning when enabling the service —
    accurate, and exactly why this app is personal-use.

  notificationTimeout — minimum interval (ms) between events of the same
    type delivered to us. 100ms coalesces event bursts during transitions
    without letting a blocked screen linger.

  description — shown to the user in the accessibility settings detail
    page for this service. Must be a string resource, not a literal.

  settingsActivity is omitted: the service has no configuration screen of
  its own (the blocklist lives in config/blocklist.ts).

  packageNames is omitted deliberately: setting it would filter events to a
  hardcoded package list at the system level, but our blocklist is dynamic
  (SharedPreferences), so we receive events from all packages and filter in
  onAccessibilityEvent instead.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
  android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeWindowStateChanged|typeWindowContentChanged"
  android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
  android:accessibilityFlags="flagDefault|flagReportViewIds|flagIncludeNotImportantViews"
  android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true"
  android:notificationTimeout="100"
  android:description="@string/fallow_accessibility_service_description" />
